# judge, expect and refuse are ordinary builtins that answer with a Furball,
# and they work wherever a program is written — not only under `meow test`.
#
# They used to compile to `meow_testing.Judge(...)` in a file that never
# imported the package, so `meow run` answered with raw Go: `undefined:
# meow_testing`, naming no line of the .nyan file. The checker accepted them
# everywhere and the interpreter ran them everywhere, so the CLI was the odd one
# out (#151). What this file prints is what the interpreter prints for it, byte
# for byte.

# An assertion that holds answers with catnap, as it does under `meow test`,
# where only the enclosing test's PASS line is printed.
nya(judge(1 == 1, "one is one"))
nya(expect(2 + 2, 4, "two and two"))
nya(refuse(1 == 2, "one is not two"))

# One that fails is a Furball, so `~>` can catch it and the message is the one
# the assertion was given.
nya(judge(1 == 2, "one is not two") ~> "caught the judge")
nya(expect(2 + 2, 5, "two and two are not five") ~> "caught the expect")
nya(refuse(1 == 1, "one is one after all") ~> "caught the refuse")

# Held as a value, an assertion is still the assertion — the wrapper #152 gave
# every builtin needs the same import.
nyan held = judge
nya(held(1 == 1, "still one"))

# Inside a function, where the Furball propagates as any other does.
meow check(n int) furball {
  bring judge(n > 0, "n must be positive")
}
nya(check(1))
nya(check(-1) ~> "caught from a function")

# seed states one entry of a fuzz corpus. Only `meow test -fuzz` has anything to
# do with one, and everywhere else it answers with catnap — but its arguments
# are arguments, and Meow evaluates those. Codegen used to splice them into
# nothing at all, so a side effect written in one never happened while the
# interpreter ran it.
nya(seed(nya("evaluated")))

# An argument that fails is passed on, the way nya passes one on, so a seed
# whose argument failed says so rather than answering catnap as if nothing had
# happened. Both backends call the same runtime function for this.
nya(seed(judge(1 == 2, "a seed argument failed")) ~> "caught the seed argument")
